From: Aadharsh Pannirselvam <aadharsh@archetype.fund>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 10:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] gdf: run the frame pass across cores

The work item is one unit row across the full frame width, with private
scratch (lap/cls/err) and line-save buffers, and a footprint: the rows of
the guided copy it may read or rewrite. Items whose footprints don't
overlap run at once, so they are packed greedily into phases and each
phase runs on Grand Central Dispatch. A 1600x2400 frame with 4 tile rows
packs into 3 phases, because each tile row ends in a short stripe whose
footprint reaches the next tile row's first one. (The first version of
this patch ran even rows then odd ones and fell back to serial when that
failed, which it did on every frame with more than one tile row.)

GDF on that frame: 26 ms on one core, 3.7 ms across them. AVM_GDF_ORIG=1
selects the original pass and AVM_GDF_SERIAL=1 this one in order; 25 of
25 decodes bit-exact against pristine AVM in every mode.

---
--- a/av2/common/gdf.c
+++ b/av2/common/gdf.c
@@ -526,7 +526,7 @@
   return qp_idx_base;
 }
 
-void gdf_filter_frame(AV2_COMMON *cm) {
+void gdf_filter_frame_orig(AV2_COMMON *cm) {
   uint16_t *const rec_pnt = cm->cur_frame->buf.buffers[AVM_PLANE_Y];
   const int rec_stride = cm->cur_frame->buf.y_stride;
 
@@ -715,5 +715,378 @@
     }
   }
 }
